BANK.push(
{"id":"m1","d":2,"q":"A defense contractor is designing a document management system for classified material. The primary requirement is that users cleared at SECRET must never be able to write information into a document classified CONFIDENTIAL, even accidentally by copy and paste. Which security model property addresses this requirement?","o":["The Bell-LaPadula *-property (no write down)","The Bell-LaPadula simple security property (no read up)","The Biba simple integrity axiom (no read down)","The Clark-Wilson separation of duty enforcement rule"],"a":[0],"e":"Bell-LaPadula is a confidentiality model. The *-property prevents a subject at a higher level from writing to a lower level, which would leak data downward. The simple security property prevents reading up. Biba is about integrity, not leakage."},
{"id":"m2","d":2,"q":"A pharmaceutical company's laboratory information system must ensure that test results cannot be altered except through validated application transactions, and that the person entering a result cannot also approve it. Which security model BEST describes this design?","o":["Clark-Wilson, using well-formed transactions on constrained data items, the access triple, and separation of duties","Biba, using integrity levels on subjects and objects so that low-integrity users can't write to high-integrity results","Brewer-Nash, using dynamic conflict-of-interest classes so that the analyst who enters a result can't later approve it","Bell-LaPadula, using security labels and clearances so that results are only visible to authorized laboratory staff"],"a":[0],"e":"Clark-Wilson enforces integrity through well-formed transactions (transformation procedures) on constrained data items, the access triple (subject-program-object), and separation of duties. Biba uses levels rather than transactions, and Brewer-Nash handles conflicts of interest."},
{"id":"m3","d":2,"q":"A consulting firm advises competing banks. Once a consultant accesses Bank A's files, the system must prevent that consultant from accessing Bank B's files, though they may still access an unrelated retailer's files. Which model implements this?","o":["Brewer-Nash (Chinese Wall)","Graham-Denning","Harrison-Ruzzo-Ullman","Lipner integrity model"],"a":[0],"e":"Brewer-Nash grants access dynamically based on what the subject has already accessed, preventing access to datasets in the same conflict-of-interest class. Graham-Denning and HRU deal with how rights are created and transferred; Lipner combines BLP and Biba."},
{"id":"m4","d":2,"q":"An architect is reviewing a high-assurance system design in which a sensor at a low-integrity level must not be able to modify the configuration of a high-integrity control process. Which rule enforces this?","o":["Biba *-integrity axiom (no write up)","Biba simple integrity axiom (no read down)","Bell-LaPadula simple security property (no read up)","Bell-LaPadula strong star property"],"a":[0],"e":"Biba's *-integrity axiom prevents a lower-integrity subject from writing to a higher-integrity object. The simple integrity axiom stops a high-integrity subject reading low-integrity data. BLP addresses confidentiality."},
{"id":"m5","d":2,"q":"An enterprise architecture team uses TOGAF. The security architect wants to ensure security requirements are established before the target business and information systems architectures are developed. In which ADM phase should security drivers and principles FIRST be captured?","o":["Preliminary phase and Phase A","Phase D (Technology Architecture)","Phase G (Implementation Governance)","Phase H (Architecture Change Management)"],"a":[0],"e":"Security principles, drivers, and stakeholder concerns belong in the Preliminary phase and Architecture Vision so that they shape Business, Data, Application, and Technology architectures. Adding them in Phase D or later is bolting on."},
{"id":"m6","d":2,"q":"During threat modeling of a new microservice, the team finds that the service trusts a role claim inside a client-supplied token without verifying the token signature. An attacker could edit the claim from 'viewer' to 'admin' and gain administrative functions. Using STRIDE, which threat category BEST describes the root weakness, and which control addresses it?","o":["Tampering with the token, so validate the token's signature and issuer before trusting any claim it contains","Spoofing of the user's identity; add a CAPTCHA to the login page so automated tools can't obtain tokens","Repudiation of administrative actions; add detailed audit logging of every request the service processes","Denial of service against the service; rate limit requests per client so that attackers can't flood it"],"a":[0],"e":"The attacker modifies data the system trusts: tampering, which violates integrity. Elevation of privilege is the consequence, but the root weakness is accepting an unsigned, modifiable token, so signature and issuer validation fixes it. CAPTCHA, logging, and rate limiting don't stop claim modification."},
{"id":"m7","d":2,"q":"A security architect wants a threat modeling approach that starts from business objectives, incorporates attack simulation, and produces risk-ranked results aligned to business impact. Which methodology BEST fits?","o":["PASTA","STRIDE per element on the data flow diagram","DREAD scoring of each identified threat","LINDDUN privacy threat analysis"],"a":[0],"e":"PASTA is a seven-stage, risk-centric method that begins with business objectives and ends with risk and impact analysis. STRIDE is a threat taxonomy, DREAD is a scoring model, and LINDDUN focuses on privacy threats."},
{"id":"m8","d":2,"q":"A privacy engineering team is modeling a new mobile health app and wants a threat framework focused on threats such as linkability, identifiability, and detectability of users. Which should they use?","o":["LINDDUN","STRIDE","OCTAVE Allegro","VAST"],"a":[0],"e":"LINDDUN covers Linking, Identifying, Non-repudiation, Detecting, Data disclosure, Unawareness, and Non-compliance: privacy-specific threats. STRIDE covers security threats, OCTAVE is an organizational risk method, and VAST is for scaling threat modeling in agile teams."},
{"id":"m9","d":2,"q":"An architect is designing a system in which every access to every object must be checked against the access policy, including cached and repeated requests. Which design principle does this describe?","o":["Complete mediation","Least common mechanism","Economy of mechanism","Fail-safe defaults"],"a":[0],"e":"Complete mediation (Saltzer and Schroeder) requires that every access be checked for authority, not just the first. Caching authorization decisions can violate it if permissions change. Least common mechanism minimizes shared mechanisms, economy of mechanism keeps designs simple, and fail-safe defaults base access on permission rather than exclusion."},
{"id":"m10","d":2,"q":"A design review finds that a single shared authentication library is used by both the internet-facing customer portal and the internal administrative console. A flaw in the library would compromise both. Which principle is MOST relevant?","o":["Least common mechanism","Open design","Psychological acceptability","Separation of privilege"],"a":[0],"e":"Least common mechanism says to minimize mechanisms shared by different users or subsystems, because each shared mechanism is a potential path for compromise across them. Reuse has benefits, but critical shared components need extra assurance."},
{"id":"m11","d":2,"q":"A security architect is explaining the reference monitor concept to a development team. Which set of properties MUST a reference validation mechanism have?","o":["It must be tamperproof, always invoked, and small enough to be completely analyzed and tested for correctness","Encrypted, redundant across two sites, and implemented in dedicated hardware rather than in software","Open source, independently certified to a high EAL, and executed in user space for easier patching","Capable of role-based, attribute-based, and discretionary access control within a single policy engine"],"a":[0],"e":"The reference monitor must be tamperproof, always invoked (non-bypassable), and verifiable (small enough to analyze). The security kernel implements it. Hardware placement, open source, and specific access models aren't requirements of the concept."},
{"id":"m12","d":2,"q":"An organization is defining its security architecture using the Zachman Framework. Which statement BEST describes how Zachman supports security architecture?","o":["A classification schema that organizes artifacts by perspective and by interrogative (what, how, where, who, when, why)","An iterative, step-by-step method for developing an enterprise architecture through a defined set of phases","A quantitative risk method that expresses the security of each architecture artifact as an expected monetary loss","A catalog of security controls mapped to regulatory requirements and organized by layer of the enterprise"],"a":[0],"e":"Zachman is a two-dimensional taxonomy: rows are perspectives (planner, owner, designer, builder, subcontractor, user) and columns are interrogatives. It describes what artifacts exist, not how to create them. SABSA adapted it for security. TOGAF's ADM is a process."},
{"id":"m13","d":2,"q":"A security architect must verify that every security requirement in the requirements specification is addressed by a design element and covered by a test case. Which artifact BEST supports this?","o":["A requirements traceability matrix","A data flow diagram annotated with trust boundaries, showing where each security control is placed","A threat model listing every identified threat, the affected component, and the planned mitigation","A risk register recording each risk's owner, rating, treatment decision, and target completion date"],"a":[0],"e":"A traceability matrix links requirements to design components and to verification activities, revealing gaps in both directions. DFDs, threat models, and risk registers feed requirements but don't trace them to tests."},
{"id":"m14","d":2,"q":"In SABSA, the architect builds a 'business attributes profile' with attributes such as 'available,' 'confidential,' 'auditable,' and 'compliant,' each with a measurable definition. What is the PRIMARY purpose of this profile?","o":["To turn business requirements into normalized, measurable security attributes that trace through every layer","To list the security products, tools, and services that will be deployed when the physical layer is designed","To define the roles, responsibilities, and escalation paths the operations team will use during security incidents","To replace the formal risk assessment, since the attribute profile already captures the business's main security concerns"],"a":[0],"e":"The business attributes profile is SABSA's core link between business needs and security design. Each attribute has a definition and a metric, enabling traceability and measurable performance targets. It's a conceptual-layer artifact, not a product list."},
{"id":"m15","d":2,"q":"An architect is assessing a proposed design with the help of an attack tree. The root node is 'Exfiltrate customer database.' Which statement BEST describes how attack trees support architectural decisions?","o":["They split the goal into OR and AND sub-goals, revealing the cheapest or likeliest paths so controls can break the most of them","They rank the system's vulnerabilities by CVSS base score, so that the most severe issues are always patched before less severe ones","They model how data moves between processes, stores, and external entities, which makes the system's trust boundaries visible","They calculate the annualized loss expectancy for each asset in the design, so that controls can be justified by cost"],"a":[0],"e":"Attack trees decompose goals into AND/OR nodes, and annotating nodes with cost, skill, or likelihood shows which paths are cheapest. Controls placed at shared nodes break many paths. DFDs show trust boundaries; CVSS and ALE are different tools."},
{"id":"m16","d":2,"q":"A designer proposes that an application deny all access unless a rule explicitly permits it, and that if the policy server is unreachable, access requests are denied. Which TWO design principles does this proposal embody?","o":["Fail-safe defaults and fail secure","Open design and least common mechanism","Economy of mechanism and psychological acceptability","Separation of privilege and complete mediation"],"a":[0],"e":"Default deny is the fail-safe defaults principle, and denying access when the policy server is unavailable is failing secure (closed). The others are valid principles but don't describe this behavior."},
{"id":"m17","d":2,"q":"A medical device manufacturer must decide whether its infusion pump should fail open or fail closed when the authentication service is unreachable. Clinicians must be able to stop or adjust an infusion during an emergency. What is the BEST architectural approach?","o":["Keep safety-critical functions available locally in a restricted, audited emergency mode, while other functions fail closed","Fail closed for every function on the pump, since a security control must never be weakened, even during a clinical emergency","Fail open for every function on the pump, since availability always outweighs confidentiality and integrity in a healthcare setting","Remove authentication from the pump entirely, so that the device no longer depends on the authentication service being reachable"],"a":[0],"e":"Safety and security must be balanced. Patient safety requires local availability of critical functions, with compensating controls such as a restricted mode and full audit. A blanket choice either way creates unacceptable risk."},
{"id":"m18","d":2,"q":"A security architect is asked to validate that a vendor's product meets a specific set of security requirements for a government agency. The vendor provides a Common Criteria certificate at EAL4+ against a Protection Profile. What should the architect check FIRST?","o":["Whether the Protection Profile, Security Target scope, and evaluated configuration match the agency's needs","Whether EAL4+ is higher than EAL3, since a higher assurance level proves the product is more secure overall","Whether the evaluation was performed by a laboratory located in the United States rather than another CCRA nation","Whether the vendor also holds an ISO 27001 certificate covering its product development and support processes"],"a":[0],"e":"Common Criteria certification only covers the Target of Evaluation as defined in the Security Target, in the evaluated configuration. A high EAL against the wrong PP or scope doesn't meet your needs. EAL measures assurance depth, not 'how secure'."},
{"id":"m19","d":2,"q":"An architect is evaluating design alternatives using a security pattern catalog. One pattern places a hardened, single-purpose component between an untrusted network and an internal service, performing protocol validation and authentication. Which pattern is this?","o":["Secure proxy (gateway / policy enforcement point)","Single access point with an internal checkpoint","Bulkhead isolation","Circuit breaker"],"a":[0],"e":"A secure proxy or gateway mediates traffic between zones and enforces policy. The bulkhead and circuit breaker are resilience patterns. 'Single access point' is related but describes a single entry, not validation between zones."},
{"id":"m20","d":2,"q":"A company's security architecture includes a reference architecture for cloud workloads. A project team asks why they must follow it when their design 'is secure enough.' What is the PRIMARY value of a reference architecture?","o":["It is a pre-approved, reusable design that embeds standards and lessons learned, reducing design risk and review effort","It guarantees that any system built according to it will pass audit and won't be breached, regardless of how it is operated","It removes the need for project-level threat modeling, because the threats have already been addressed in the reference design","It replaces the organization's security policies and standards for any project that chooses to adopt the reference architecture"],"a":[0],"e":"Reference architectures encode approved patterns, speeding delivery and improving consistency. They don't guarantee security and don't replace project-specific threat modeling for unique risks."},
{"id":"m21","d":2,"q":"A security architect is performing a gap analysis between the current and target security architecture. Which output is MOST important for planning?","o":["A prioritized gap list with risks and a roadmap","A complete inventory of every server and application","A list of all licensed security products","The latest penetration test results"],"a":[0],"e":"Gap analysis identifies the difference between current and target, then prioritizes it into a roadmap. Inventories and test results are inputs, not the gap analysis output."},
{"id":"m22","d":2,"q":"Which statement BEST describes the difference between verification and validation of a security architecture?","o":["Verification confirms it was built to its specification; validation confirms the right thing was built for stakeholders","Verification is performed by the end users of the system, while validation is performed by the developers who built it","Verification is concerned with whether the system meets performance targets, while validation is concerned with security","There is no meaningful difference, and architecture frameworks use the two terms interchangeably for the same activity"],"a":[0],"e":"Verification: 'Did we build it right?' (conformance to spec). Validation: 'Did we build the right thing?' (meets needs). A system can pass verification against a flawed spec and still fail validation."},
{"id":"m23","d":2,"q":"A government agency wants to model the architecture of a mission system, including operational, systems, and services viewpoints for defense acquisition. Which framework is MOST appropriate?","o":["DoDAF","SABSA","ITIL 4","COBIT 2019"],"a":[0],"e":"DoDAF (and MODAF/NAF in allied nations) is the US Department of Defense Architecture Framework with operational, systems, and service viewpoints. SABSA is for security architecture, ITIL for service management, and COBIT for IT governance."},
{"id":"m24","d":2,"q":"A threat modeling session uses a data flow diagram (DFD). Which DFD element is MOST important for identifying where security controls are needed?","o":["Trust boundaries","Data stores","Processes","External entities"],"a":[0],"e":"Trust boundaries mark where authentication, authorization, validation, and encryption are needed. Every flow crossing a boundary must be examined. The other elements matter, but boundaries drive control placement."},
{"id":"m25","d":2,"q":"A security model is required that formally defines how subjects can create, delete, and transfer access rights, using eight protection rules. Which model is this?","o":["Graham-Denning","Take-Grant","Bell-LaPadula","Brewer-Nash"],"a":[0],"e":"Graham-Denning defines eight rules covering creating and deleting objects and subjects and reading, granting, deleting, and transferring access rights. Take-Grant uses a directed graph with take, grant, create, and remove operations."},
{"id":"m26","d":2,"q":"A retail company's architecture team has drafted a target-state design for a new omnichannel platform. Before approving it, the security architect wants independent assurance that the design actually mitigates the threats identified in the threat model. The platform has not been built yet. Which activity is MOST appropriate at this stage?","o":["A structured design review tracing each identified threat to the controls that mitigate it, and flagging untreated threats","A penetration test of the proposed platform, carried out by an external firm against a staging copy of the current website","A vulnerability scan of the cloud environment that will host the new platform, run once infrastructure has been provisioned","A source code review of whichever components the development team has already committed to the shared repository"],"a":[0],"e":"Before build, assurance comes from design review: walking threats through to controls. Pen tests and scans need a built system, and partial code review misses design-level gaps."},
{"id":"m27","d":2,"q":"An architect proposes splitting a monolithic banking application into microservices. A reviewer argues that the new design increases the attack surface. Which design measure BEST addresses that concern while keeping the benefits of decomposition?","o":["Define trust boundaries and enforce mutual authentication, least-privilege authorization, and policy at each service interface","Keep all of the microservices on a single flat network segment so that the firewall rules between them stay simple to manage","Expose every microservice directly to the internet so that each one can be scaled and load-balanced independently of the others","Use a single shared database credential for all of the microservices, to reduce the number of secrets that must be managed"],"a":[0],"e":"Decomposition adds interfaces, so each needs authentication and authorization (for example, mTLS and service identity). Flat networks, internet exposure, and shared credentials all expand the blast radius."},
{"id":"m28","d":2,"q":"A financial trading firm uses a design in which two independent administrators must each enter half of a key to unlock the master signing key in an HSM. Which security design principle is being applied?","o":["Separation of privilege (dual control)","Least common mechanism","Economy of mechanism","Open design (Kerckhoffs's principle)"],"a":[0],"e":"Requiring two parties for a sensitive action is separation of privilege, implemented as dual control with split knowledge. It prevents a single insider from acting alone."},
{"id":"m29","d":2,"q":"A security architect is choosing a cipher for a new product. A vendor proposes a proprietary encryption algorithm, claiming it is more secure because its design is secret. Which principle does this conflict with, and why does it matter?","o":["Open design (Kerckhoffs's principle)","Least privilege: a secret algorithm grants the key holder too many rights","Psychological acceptability: developers will find it harder to use","Complete mediation: a proprietary cipher can't check every access"],"a":[0],"e":"Kerckhoffs's principle: a system should be secure even if everything except the key is public. Proprietary 'security through obscurity' algorithms routinely break once examined. Use vetted, standardized algorithms."},
{"id":"m30","d":2,"q":"Users at a company keep writing complex passwords on sticky notes because the password policy requires 20 random characters changed every 30 days. Which design principle is the policy violating, and what is the security consequence?","o":["Psychological acceptability","Economy of mechanism; the policy is too simple to resist cracking","Fail-safe defaults; users get access before proving identity","Separation of privilege; one password unlocks many systems"],"a":[0],"e":"Psychological acceptability (Saltzer and Schroeder) says security mechanisms shouldn't make resources harder to access than they would be without them, or users will work around them. Sticky notes are the workaround."},
{"id":"m31","d":2,"q":"A security architect is designing a secure enclave for processing sensitive analytics. The design uses a hardware trusted execution environment (TEE) with remote attestation. What does remote attestation PRIMARILY provide?","o":["Cryptographic evidence to a remote party that specific, unmodified code is running in a genuine TEE before secrets are released","Encryption of all data in transit between the enclave and the remote analytics client, using hardware-generated keys","Protection against denial-of-service attacks targeting the enclave's host, using hardware-enforced resource quotas","Automatic patching of the enclave's software whenever new vulnerabilities are disclosed by the hardware vendor"],"a":[0],"e":"Attestation proves the identity and integrity of the code and platform (measurements signed by hardware-rooted keys), so a relying party can decide whether to provision secrets. It isn't transport encryption, DoS protection, or patching."},
{"id":"m32","d":2,"q":"An architecture review board must evaluate two competing designs for a customer identity platform. Design A has more features; Design B has fewer components, a smaller codebase, and fewer integration points. All else being equal, which principle favors Design B?","o":["Economy of mechanism","Least privilege","Defense in depth","Fail-safe defaults"],"a":[0],"e":"Economy of mechanism: keep designs as simple and small as possible, since simpler systems are easier to analyze and have fewer flaws. Defense in depth may sometimes favor more layers, but that isn't the property described."},
{"id":"m33","d":2,"q":"An organization runs a security architecture assessment and finds that a perimeter firewall is the only control protecting a database of customer records. Internal users, VPN users, and partner connections all reach the database directly once inside. Which principle is MOST clearly missing?","o":["Defense in depth","Open design","Psychological acceptability","Least common mechanism"],"a":[0],"e":"A single control layer means one failure leads to compromise. Defense in depth adds independent layers (segmentation, database access control, encryption, monitoring) so a single failure isn't catastrophic."},
{"id":"m34","d":2,"q":"A government agency's high-assurance system must prevent information flows between security levels via covert channels. A review finds that a low-level process can infer high-level activity by measuring CPU cache timing. Which type of covert channel is this, and what is a typical architectural mitigation?","o":["A covert timing channel; mitigate by partitioning or flushing shared resources and adding timing noise","A covert storage channel; mitigate by encrypting all files at rest so that low-level processes can't read them","An overt channel; mitigate by requiring stronger authentication for all processes running at the low security level","A network side channel; mitigate by encrypting all traffic between the high and low security levels with TLS"],"a":[0],"e":"Measuring timing of a shared resource is a covert timing channel. Mitigations include resource partitioning, cache flushing on context switch, and constant-time operations. Storage channels communicate through shared storage attributes, such as file existence or size."},
{"id":"m35","d":2,"q":"A company wants to model the security of an IoT product line in which millions of devices connect to a cloud backend. The architect needs an approach that scales threat modeling across many agile teams and integrates with DevOps pipelines. Which methodology is MOST suitable?","o":["VAST, with application and operational threat models","OCTAVE, run as facilitated organizational workshops","Manual STRIDE analysis by the central security team, once per product line","A single attack tree for the entire product line"],"a":[0],"e":"VAST was designed to scale threat modeling across agile enterprises, using separate application and operational threat models and automation. OCTAVE is organizational, and a one-time central analysis doesn't keep up with agile change."},
{"id":"m36","d":2,"q":"An architect is reviewing the security of a legacy mainframe application. There is no documentation. Which approach is MOST effective for creating an accurate security model of the current state?","o":["Reverse-engineer it from interviews, configurations, and data flows, validated by traffic and log analysis","Assume the application follows the mainframe vendor's reference architecture, and use that as the current-state model","Skip modeling the current state and go straight to the target state, since the current state will be replaced anyway","Run an authenticated vulnerability scan of the mainframe and treat the resulting findings as the architecture model"],"a":[0],"e":"Current-state modeling with no documentation needs triangulation: people, configurations, data flows, and observed behavior. Assuming a reference architecture or skipping current state leads to invalid gap analysis."},
{"id":"m37","d":2,"q":"Which statement BEST describes the 'security kernel' in a trusted computing base?","o":["The TCB elements implementing the reference monitor","All software on the system, including applications","The user-facing security settings in the OS","The cryptographic library used by applications"],"a":[0],"e":"The security kernel is the part of the TCB that implements the reference monitor: mediating all access between subjects and objects. The TCB is everything trusted to enforce the policy; the kernel is its core."},
{"id":"m38","d":2,"q":"An architect is designing a multi-tenant SaaS platform. One requirement states that 'a defect in one tenant's custom workflow must not affect other tenants.' Which architectural approach BEST meets it?","o":["Isolate tenant execution with per-tenant sandboxes or compute boundaries and resource quotas (bulkhead)","Run every tenant's workflows in one shared process, with thorough input validation on each workflow definition","Give each tenant a separate, branded login page so that its users never interact with other tenants' users","Encrypt each tenant's data with a separate key, so that a defect in one workflow can't affect another tenant"],"a":[0],"e":"The requirement is fault and resource isolation, which the bulkhead pattern provides. Per-tenant keys protect confidentiality but don't stop a runaway workflow from consuming shared resources. A shared process allows cross-tenant impact."},
{"id":"m39","d":2,"q":"An organization's architecture process requires that security controls be selected based on the system's categorization, then tailored, implemented, assessed, and continuously monitored. Which framework does this describe?","o":["NIST RMF (SP 800-37)","ISO/IEC 27005","COBIT 2019","ITIL 4"],"a":[0],"e":"The NIST RMF steps are Prepare, Categorize, Select, Implement, Assess, Authorize, and Monitor. ISO 27005 is risk management guidance, COBIT governs IT broadly, and ITIL manages services."},
{"id":"m40","d":2,"q":"Under the NIST RMF, which role formally accepts the residual risk of operating a federal information system and grants authority to operate?","o":["The Authorizing Official","The System Owner","The Information System Security Officer","The Security Control Assessor"],"a":[0],"e":"The Authorizing Official (AO) is a senior official who accepts the risk and issues the ATO. The System Owner manages the system, the ISSO handles day-to-day security, and assessors evaluate controls."},
{"id":"m41","d":2,"q":"A security architect is categorizing a new federal system under FIPS 199. The system has a confidentiality impact of moderate, integrity of high, and availability of low. What is the overall security categorization using the high water mark?","o":["High","Moderate","Low","It can't be determined without a full risk assessment"],"a":[0],"e":"FIPS 199 and FIPS 200 use the high water mark: the overall categorization equals the highest impact level across the three objectives. Integrity is high, so the system is high and uses the high baseline in SP 800-53B."},
{"id":"m42","d":2,"q":"A team is designing an authorization architecture for a large enterprise and wants to separate the decision of whether access is allowed from its enforcement. Which architectural components implement this separation?","o":["A policy decision point and policy enforcement points","A firewall in front of each application and an IDS behind it","A directory server and a certificate authority","A load balancer and a reverse proxy"],"a":[0],"e":"The PDP/PEP split (from XACML and NIST zero trust, SP 800-207) separates decision from enforcement, allowing centralized policy and distributed enforcement. Policy information points supply attributes, and the policy administration point manages policy."},
{"id":"m43","d":2,"q":"An architect is evaluating whether a new design meets the security requirement 'all administrative actions must be attributable to an individual.' Which design element is MOST necessary?","o":["Unique individual administrator accounts with strong authentication, plus tamper-evident logging tied to those identities","Shared administrator accounts with long, complex passwords that are rotated monthly and kept in a password vault","A firewall rule that restricts administrative access to the management VLAN and a small set of approved jump hosts","TLS on the administrative interface, so that administrator sessions can't be intercepted or modified in transit"],"a":[0],"e":"Attribution (accountability, non-repudiation) needs individual identity plus protected logs. Shared accounts break attribution. Network restriction and TLS are good controls but don't attribute actions."},
{"id":"m44","d":2,"q":"A security architecture review identifies that a proposed system design depends on a single certificate authority for all internal TLS, code signing, and user authentication. What is the MOST significant architectural concern?","o":["A compromise of the single CA would undermine all three trust functions at once, so issuing CAs should be separated by purpose","The CA will eventually run out of certificate serial numbers if it issues certificates for all three purposes","Users will find it hard to know which certificates to trust when one CA is used for several different purposes","Under the X.509 standard, TLS server certificates can't be issued by the same CA that issues code signing certificates"],"a":[0],"e":"Separating issuing CAs by purpose (with a common offline root) limits the impact of a compromise and allows different policies. There's no technical prohibition on mixing, but it's poor design."},
{"id":"m45","d":2,"q":"A company wants assurance that the security architecture of its payment platform remains aligned with business requirements as both change over time. Which practice BEST provides this?","o":["Governance with periodic reassessment, change-triggered reviews, and traceability to business attributes","An annual penetration test of the payment platform by an independent firm, with every finding tracked to closure","A single, thorough architecture review at the start of the project, with formal sign-off by the review board","Continuous vulnerability scanning of every platform component, with weekly summary reports sent to management"],"a":[0],"e":"Alignment over time needs governance: reassessment and change-triggered reviews, tied back to business requirements. Pen tests and scans find technical flaws, not alignment drift."},
{"id":"m46","d":2,"q":"A security architect is designing a system using the Lipner integrity model. What does Lipner's model combine, and why?","o":["Bell-LaPadula and Biba","Clark-Wilson and Brewer-Nash, covering conflicts of interest","Take-Grant and HRU, covering the safety problem","RBAC and ABAC, covering dynamic authorization"],"a":[0],"e":"Lipner applied both BLP (confidentiality) and Biba (integrity) lattices to model commercial requirements, separating development from production and controlling how software moves between them."},
{"id":"m47","d":2,"q":"Which statement BEST describes the 'safety problem' identified by the Harrison-Ruzzo-Ullman (HRU) model?","o":["For general access-matrix systems, deciding whether a right can leak to an unauthorized subject is undecidable","Access control systems can't prevent the physical theft of devices that store protected information","Encryption can't guarantee the safety of data while it is being processed in memory by an application","A reference monitor can't be implemented securely in software and must be built into dedicated hardware"],"a":[0],"e":"HRU showed that, for general protection systems, determining whether a right can ever reach an unauthorized subject (safety) is undecidable. It's decidable only for restricted cases (for example, mono-operational commands). This motivates simpler, analyzable access models."},
{"id":"m48","d":2,"q":"An architect is creating a security architecture for a hospital's clinical systems and wants each architecture decision to reference business drivers. In SABSA, which matrix or tool explicitly supports this traceability?","o":["The SABSA matrix, with two-way traceability from contextual requirements to operational components","The STRIDE threat matrix, which maps each design element to the threat categories that apply to it","The RACI matrix, which records who is responsible, accountable, consulted, and informed for each decision","The CVSS scoring matrix, which rates each identified weakness so decisions can be prioritized consistently"],"a":[0],"e":"SABSA's core feature is two-way traceability: every control traces up to a business requirement (completeness) and every requirement down to a control (justification). STRIDE, RACI, and CVSS don't provide this."},
{"id":"m49","d":2,"q":"An organization's architecture team builds a 'security capability model' listing capabilities such as identity governance, threat detection, data protection, and incident response, each rated for maturity. What is the PRIMARY architectural use of such a model?","o":["Find capability gaps and plan investment","Document the configuration of each security tool","Assign employees to individual security tasks","Calculate the ALE of each business process"],"a":[0],"e":"Capability models describe what the organization must be able to do, independent of technology. Rating maturity shows gaps and guides investment. They're not configuration documents or staffing plans."},
{"id":"m50","d":2,"q":"A financial institution is designing a new real-time payments system. The threat model identifies that an insider at the payment operations center could initiate fraudulent payments. Which combination of design controls BEST addresses this threat?","o":["Maker-checker workflows, transaction limits, anomaly detection on payments, and immutable audit logs","Strong perimeter firewalls and an inline intrusion prevention system between the operations center and the internet","Full-disk encryption on every operations center workstation, so payment data is protected if a device is stolen","Endpoint antivirus with daily signature updates on every operations center workstation and payment server"],"a":[0],"e":"Insider fraud is addressed by requiring multiple people (maker-checker), limiting impact (limits), detecting anomalies, and keeping evidence (immutable logs). Perimeter and endpoint controls address external threats."},
{"id":"m51","d":2,"q":"A security architect is asked to evaluate a vendor's claim that its new access gateway implements zero trust. The gateway authenticates users once at login and then allows unrestricted access to all internal applications for eight hours. Which zero trust tenet does the design MOST clearly fail?","o":["Access to each resource should be granted per session, evaluated dynamically using current signals","All data sources and computing services in the enterprise should be treated as resources that need protection","All communication should be secured regardless of network location, including traffic on the internal network","The enterprise should collect as much information as possible about asset state and use it to improve its posture"],"a":[0],"e":"NIST SP 800-207 requires per-session, per-resource access with dynamic policy. A single login granting broad access for hours is essentially a VPN model. The other tenets may or may not be met, but per-resource dynamic evaluation is clearly missing."},
{"id":"m52","d":2,"q":"An architect is reviewing a design where a mobile app talks directly to a backend database through an exposed database port, with authorization logic implemented in the app. What is the MOST fundamental architectural flaw?","o":["Security enforcement sits on the client, which the attacker controls, rather than on a trusted server tier","The database is listening on its default port, which attackers scan for, so it should be moved to a non-standard port","The mobile app's authorization logic is readable after decompilation, so the app should be obfuscated before release","The shared application account uses a weak database password, which should be replaced with a long random one"],"a":[0],"e":"Client-side enforcement can be bypassed by anyone who controls the client. Authorization must happen in a trusted tier (an API layer). Port changes and obfuscation are obscurity, and a stronger shared password is still embedded in the client."},
{"id":"m53","d":2,"q":"A security architect needs to choose an access control model for a hospital system where access should depend on the clinician's role, the patient's location, the time of day, and whether there is an active treatment relationship. Which model BEST supports this?","o":["Attribute-based access control (ABAC)","Role-based access control (RBAC)","Mandatory access control (MAC) with classification labels","Discretionary access control (DAC) by record owners"],"a":[0],"e":"Context such as location, time, and relationship are attributes, so ABAC fits naturally. Pure RBAC would need a role explosion to express them. MAC uses fixed labels, and DAC leaves decisions to individuals, which doesn't scale or meet policy."},
{"id":"m54","d":2,"q":"A design team is debating whether to perform threat modeling once during design or continuously. The system will be deployed through a CI/CD pipeline with weekly releases. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Keep the threat model as a living artifact, updated when architecture, data flows, or trust boundaries change","Build one comprehensive threat model before the first release and rely on penetration tests to catch later design changes","Perform a complete, from-scratch threat model for every weekly release, regardless of how small the change is","Skip threat modeling, because automated security testing in the CI/CD pipeline will find the same threats more efficiently"],"a":[0],"e":"Threat models go stale as systems change. Updating them on meaningful change (not every release) balances effort and value. Automated testing finds implementation bugs, not design flaws, and a single upfront model misses later changes."},
{"id":"m55","d":2,"q":"An architect is designing a system that processes highly sensitive data and uses a layered model where inner rings have more privilege than outer rings, and calls from outer to inner rings go through controlled gates. Which concept is this?","o":["Protection rings","Concentric VLAN segmentation","Onion routing","Layered perimeter firewalls"],"a":[0],"e":"Protection rings (for example, x86 rings 0-3, Multics) are hierarchical privilege domains. Transitions to more privileged rings go through gates such as system calls. They're a host-level isolation mechanism, not a network design."},
{"id":"m56","d":2,"q":"A security architect is asked to assess whether a proposed design for a payments API meets the organization's non-repudiation requirement for high-value transactions. The design uses TLS with server certificates and API keys for clients. What is the MOST significant gap?","o":["API keys are shared secrets, so high-value transactions should be digitally signed with private keys that only the client holds","TLS should be upgraded to version 1.3, since its handshake binds each transaction to the authenticated client identity","API keys should be rotated more often, since a short key lifetime proves that only the current client could have used it","The server certificate should be issued by a public CA rather than an internal CA, so that third parties can verify it"],"a":[0],"e":"Non-repudiation requires that only the originator could have produced the evidence. Shared secrets (API keys, HMAC) can be produced by either party. Digital signatures with client-held private keys provide origin proof. TLS version and CA choice don't change this."},
{"id":"m57","d":2,"q":"Which security architecture principle is MOST directly applied when an organization designs its systems so that compromise of an employee's workstation does not automatically give the attacker access to domain administrator credentials?","o":["Tiered administration with privilege separation, so that high-privilege credentials are never exposed on lower-tier systems","Open design, so that the workstation's configuration is published and reviewed by independent security researchers","Psychological acceptability, so that users are not burdened by security prompts and don't look for workarounds","Economy of mechanism, so that each workstation has fewer installed applications that could contain vulnerabilities"],"a":[0],"e":"Tiered administration (for example, Microsoft's enterprise access model) prevents high-privilege credentials from being used on, and therefore stolen from, lower-tier assets. It applies least privilege and separation of privilege to credential exposure."},
{"id":"m58","d":2,"q":"An architect is developing a security architecture for a utility company's operational technology (OT). Which reference model is MOST commonly used to define zones and conduits for OT security?","o":["ISA/IEC 62443 with the Purdue model","The OSI seven-layer model","The TCP/IP four-layer model","The Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain"],"a":[0],"e":"ISA/IEC 62443 defines zones and conduits with security levels, and is commonly paired with the Purdue model (levels 0-5) for ICS segmentation. OSI and TCP/IP are networking models; the Kill Chain describes attacker phases."},
{"id":"m59","d":2,"q":"During architecture validation, a red team finds that a firewall rule allows unintended traffic due to an overly broad object group. The design document specified the correct narrow rule. Which statement BEST describes this finding?","o":["A verification failure","A validation failure","A threat modeling failure","A governance failure"],"a":[0],"e":"The design was right but the implementation diverged, which is a verification failure (built it wrong). Validation failures mean the design itself was wrong for the need."},
{"id":"m60","d":2,"q":"A security architect must design for a system in which an outage of the authentication service would halt all hospital operations. Which architecture pattern BEST addresses this availability risk without weakening authentication?","o":["Redundant authentication across failure domains, with bounded, audited local credential caching","Disable authentication automatically during outages, so that clinicians can keep working without interruption","Replace user authentication with network location checks, since devices on the hospital network are trusted","Run a single, highly reliable authentication server backed by an extended 24x7 vendor support contract"],"a":[0],"e":"Redundancy across failure domains plus bounded offline capability keeps authentication available. Disabling auth or trusting the network fails secure design, and a single server is a single point of failure."},
{"id":"m61","d":2,"q":"An architect is defining security requirements for a new system. A stakeholder writes: 'The system must be secure.' What is the BEST way to improve this requirement?","o":["Rewrite it as specific, measurable, testable requirements tied to threats and business attributes","Leave it as written, since it captures the stakeholder's intent and experienced developers will know what it means","Replace it with a list of the security products that the system must use, such as a WAF, SIEM, and EDR agent","Remove it from the specification, since security is the responsibility of the security team, not the project"],"a":[0],"e":"Good requirements are specific, measurable, and testable. Vague requirements can't be verified or traced, product lists constrain design without stating intent, and removing security requirements guarantees gaps."},
{"id":"m62","d":2,"q":"A company's enterprise architecture defines building blocks. In TOGAF, how are architecture building blocks (ABBs) and solution building blocks (SBBs) related in a security context?","o":["ABBs define required capabilities; SBBs are the specific products or implementations that realize them","ABBs are the physical security products chosen for the solution; SBBs are the abstract capabilities they deliver","ABBs apply only to the business architecture domain, while SBBs apply only to the technology architecture domain","There is no relationship between them, because they come from different frameworks that are never used together"],"a":[0],"e":"ABBs describe required capability ('centralized key management with HSM-backed root keys'). SBBs are concrete implementations (a particular HSM and KMS). This keeps requirements separate from product choice."},
{"id":"m63","d":2,"q":"An architect wants to quantify how well the current architecture would withstand a set of realistic attack scenarios before investing in new controls. Which technique BEST supports this?","o":["Breach and attack simulation or purple team exercises mapped to MITRE ATT&CK, measuring prevention and detection coverage","A compliance gap assessment of the environment against the ISO 27001 Annex A controls, with a maturity score for each","A survey of IT and security staff asking how confident they are that the current controls would stop a real attack","A count of the security tools deployed at each layer of the architecture, compared with an industry reference model"],"a":[0],"e":"Testing realistic techniques against the live environment measures actual control effectiveness. Compliance assessments, surveys, and tool counts don't show whether attacks are blocked or detected."},
{"id":"m64","d":2,"q":"A security architect is designing role structures for an ERP system. To prevent fraud, the design must ensure no single user can both create a vendor and approve payments to that vendor. Which mechanism enforces this at design time?","o":["Static separation of duties","Dynamic separation of duties","Mandatory vacations","Job rotation"],"a":[0],"e":"Static SoD prevents conflicting role assignment entirely. Dynamic SoD allows both roles but not simultaneous activation, which is weaker for this fraud case. Vacations and rotation are detective and deterrent controls, not design-time enforcement."},
{"id":"m65","d":2,"q":"Which statement BEST describes the purpose of a security architecture 'pattern' compared to a 'reference architecture'?","o":["A pattern solves a specific recurring problem; a reference architecture combines many patterns for a domain","A pattern is mandatory for every project, while a reference architecture is an optional guide that teams may consult","A pattern is a specific security product, while a reference architecture is a policy describing how products are used","They are the same thing, and the two terms are used interchangeably by most architecture frameworks and practitioners"],"a":[0],"e":"Patterns solve specific problems (secure proxy, token exchange). Reference architectures assemble patterns into an end-to-end template for a domain (cloud landing zone, e-commerce platform)."},
{"id":"m66","d":2,"q":"A security architect discovers that a critical business process depends on an interface whose security properties are undocumented and whose owner left the company. Which architectural risk is this, and what is the FIRST step?","o":["An undocumented dependency; first assign an owner and document its flows, trust assumptions, and controls","A supply chain attack in progress; first notify law enforcement and preserve evidence from the interface","A covert channel; first block the interface at the firewall until its purpose can be fully understood","A denial-of-service risk; first add rate limiting to the interface so that it can't be overwhelmed"],"a":[0],"e":"Unknown, unowned dependencies are architectural risk. Ownership and documentation come first so the risk can be assessed. Blocking it may break a critical process, and there's no evidence of an attack."},
{"id":"m67","d":2,"q":"An architect needs to model an e-voting system where voters must be anonymous, yet the system must ensure each voter votes once and votes are counted correctly. Which pairing of properties creates the PRIMARY design tension?","o":["Anonymity versus accountability","Availability versus performance","Encryption strength versus key length","Integrity versus authenticity of the software"],"a":[0],"e":"Voting requires unlinkability of votes to voters (privacy) while verifying eligibility and preventing double voting (accountability). Cryptographic techniques such as blind signatures and mix-nets address this tension."},
{"id":"m68","d":2,"q":"A security architect is evaluating the trustworthiness of an open-source cryptographic library for use in a new product. Which factor provides the STRONGEST assurance?","o":["Independent audits, active maintenance, responsive vulnerability handling, and FIPS 140-3 validation where needed","The number of stars and forks the project has on its public repository, which shows how widely it is trusted","The fact that the source code is publicly available, which means many people have already reviewed it for flaws","A strong recommendation from experienced developers on a popular programming forum who use it in production"],"a":[0],"e":"Open source enables review but doesn't guarantee it happened. Independent audits, active maintenance, disclosure processes, and formal validation give real assurance. Popularity and availability of code are weak signals."},
{"id":"m69","d":2,"q":"A financial institution's security architecture is assessed using the ISO/IEC 21827 Systems Security Engineering Capability Maturity Model (SSE-CMM). What does this assessment measure?","o":["Security engineering process maturity","The cryptographic strength of its products","The number of vulnerabilities in its applications","Its compliance with PCI DSS"],"a":[0],"e":"SSE-CMM measures process capability for security engineering across the life cycle. It doesn't evaluate a specific product (that's Common Criteria) or compliance with a particular standard."},
{"id":"m70","d":2,"q":"An architect is designing a file-sharing system for a law firm. Each attorney should be able to share their own case files with colleagues at their discretion, but the firm requires that no files tagged 'privileged' ever be shared outside the firm. Which combination of access control approaches fits BEST?","o":["DAC for normal sharing, constrained by a mandatory label-based policy that blocks external sharing of privileged files","Pure discretionary access control, supported by regular training on how to recognize and handle privileged material","Pure mandatory access control, with every sharing decision made centrally by the IT department on request","Role-based access control with a separate role for each case, assigned to the attorneys working on that case"],"a":[0],"e":"DAC gives attorneys flexibility; a mandatory label-based overlay enforces the firm-wide rule regardless of user choice. Pure DAC relies on behavior, pure MAC is too rigid, and per-case roles explode in number."},
{"id":"m71","d":2,"q":"A security architect wants to ensure that a cloud-native design is evaluated consistently against the organization's security principles. Which approach scales BEST across hundreds of teams?","o":["Codify principles as automated policy checks on IaC and manifests, with manual review for exceptions","Require the central architecture team to manually review every design document before it can be implemented","Publish the security principles on the intranet and trust each team to apply them correctly in its own designs","Require every product team to hire its own security architect who reviews all of that team's designs"],"a":[0],"e":"Policy-as-code scales review to many teams and gives fast feedback, while humans focus on novel risk. Central manual review becomes a bottleneck, publishing alone isn't enforcement, and per-team architects are costly."},
{"id":"m72","d":2,"q":"Which BEST describes the purpose of the 'operational layer' in the SABSA framework?","o":["How the architecture is run, monitored, and maintained over time","The business drivers and context for security","The specific products and tools selected","The logical security services and their relationships"],"a":[0],"e":"SABSA's operational layer runs vertically across all others, covering security service management, operations, and continuity. Contextual covers business, logical covers services, and component covers products."},
{"id":"m73","d":2,"q":"An architect is comparing DREAD and CVSS for rating threats identified during design. What is the MOST significant limitation of DREAD that led Microsoft to stop recommending it?","o":["Subjective, inconsistent ratings","It only applies to network-layer threats","It can't be used together with STRIDE","It requires expensive commercial tools"],"a":[0],"e":"DREAD (Damage, Reproducibility, Exploitability, Affected users, Discoverability) proved subjective and inconsistent. Organizations now tend to use CVSS for vulnerabilities or risk-based ratings for design threats."},
{"id":"m74","d":2,"q":"A security architecture must support a 'need to know' requirement within a single classification level. Which mechanism in a mandatory access control system addresses this?","o":["Compartments or categories","Raising the information's classification level","Firewall rules between departments","Encryption with a single shared key"],"a":[0],"e":"In MAC lattices, labels include a level and a set of categories or compartments. A subject needs both the clearance and the compartments to access an object. Raising classification doesn't express need to know."},
{"id":"m75","d":2,"q":"An architect is designing a system where operational data must be visible to analysts but they must not be able to infer the identity of individuals from aggregated query results. Which technique BEST addresses this inference risk at the architecture level?","o":["Differential privacy or query restrictions with minimum cell sizes","Encryption of the database at rest with HSM-managed keys","Phishing-resistant MFA for all analysts","Logging of every query for later review"],"a":[0],"e":"Inference and aggregation attacks combine allowed results to reveal restricted information. Differential privacy (adding calibrated noise) and query controls such as minimum set sizes address this directly. Encryption at rest, MFA, and logging don't prevent inference."}
);
